=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The network is entirely healthy. The user's claim that super_spine_router_0 is silently dropping packets is factually incorrect; all measurements contradict it.

Direct evidence: a 5-packet ping from pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) to pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) returns 5/5 with 0% loss and ~1.3 ms RTT. A traceroute confirms the exact forwarding path: pc_0_0 → leaf_router_0_0 → spine_router_0_0 → leaf_router_0_1 → pc_0_1 (three router hops, TTL decremented from 64 to 61). super_spine_router_0 does not appear in this path at all, and that is by design.

The dc_clos_bgp topology has a single pod (one pair of leaves, one pair of spines) crowned by a super_spine. In Clos architecture the super_spine is only in the forwarding path for inter-pod traffic; intra-pod traffic — which is exactly what pc_0_0 ↔ pc_0_1 is — routes directly through the spine layer. super_spine_router_0 has both interfaces UP, an empty iptables ruleset (policy ACCEPT, zero rules), a valid BGP RIB with two equal-cost paths learned for both host prefixes, and all BGP sessions Established with both spine peers. There are no null routes, no drops, and no misconfigurations on any device in the fabric.

Root cause: there is no fault; the user misattributed the absence of super_spine from the data path to packet dropping, when in fact the Clos topology intentionally routes intra-pod traffic through the spine layer, not through the super_spine.

Corrective action: none required. Educate the ticket submitter that super_spine_router_0's role is inter-pod transit; it is correctly absent from intra-pod forwarding paths.
=== END ===